I'm sure the OP wasn't talking about pacifism as a winning strategy.
Pacifism does serve three purposes:

1) Survival tactics in tough situations.
2) A way to learn to slow down Nethack game play. This is important.
Most Nethack novices (and some vets) simply rush into every situation.
Pacifism by its very nature teaches you to slow down, avoid, and think.
This is a skillset that's really useful even in normal game play.
3) It provides an enhancement to another early game strategy, the
protection racket.

>> Use Elbereth to avoid monsters.
>
>I never have, and I just finished my 10th ascension a few nights ago.
